#!/usr/bin/env ruby

# THE OTHER HALF OF "NOTICE IT AND ACT" — `bin/qa_sweep` finds a bug by
# fuzzing; this finds one by asking GitHub what it already knows. Same
# contract shape, same reason: deterministic, mechanical, an exit code an
# agent (or a loop) reads instead of a transcript it has to parse.
#
# THE GAP THIS CLOSES. A `loop-parity/*` PR is a fix `bin/qa_sweep`'s own
# agent judged self-contained and verified LOCALLY before opening it —
# but "verified locally" and "CI's own fresh build agrees" are two
# different facts, and until this script existed nothing in the ledger
# ever checked whether they still matched. A PR could go red days later —
# a fix that happened to work on one machine's cache, a flake that wasn't,
# a merge with `main` that broke it — and the only thing that noticed was
# a human scrolling GitHub's own PR list. `QualityControl::Bug::Regress`
# already said the right word ("put a bug back when its fix stopped
# holding") and nothing had ever dispatched it — `BugCiWatch`
# (qa/bluebook/quality_control.bluebook) is the process manager that
# turns a `Clearance::Failed` into a `Bug::Regress` on its own,
# automatically, the moment a clearance is recorded for the commit a Bug
# named as its fix.
#
# THE SECOND GAP THIS CLOSES, LATER — DISCOVERY ITSELF. This script used
# to ask GitHub to GUESS which PRs were ours: `gh pr list --search
# "head:loop-parity"`, a branch-prefix convention. Checked against this
# practice's own real history, not assumed: that convention was never a
# reliable filter. #533/#532/#529/#526 are real `loop-parity/*` branches
# titled `"heki: ..."`/`"BUG#N: ..."`/`"rust: ..."` rather than `"qa: ..."`
# — a title search misses them. #534 is titled `"qa: ..."` on a branch
# that never got the `loop-parity/` prefix at all — a branch search misses
# it. It sat open and genuinely red, unseen by either, for a day. Widening
# to the union of both patterns is still a guess about every naming habit
# an agent will ever have; it only has to be wrong once more, and a draft
# PR sits outside a plain `gh pr list` by default on top of that.
#
# `QualityControl::Patch` (qa/bluebook/quality_control.bluebook, "──
# which pull requests are ours ──") is what ends the guessing for good: a
# PR is recorded, once, the moment its number/branch/commit are already
# known — `gh pr create`'s own return value, not a search result. This
# script now asks `Patch.Open` for the worklist and `gh` about each row
# BY ITS OWN NUMBER. There is no `--search` anywhere in this file any
# more, and there does not need to be: a PR this ledger does not know
# about is not ours to check — see `.claude/skills/hecks_qa/SKILL.md`'s
# own new orchestrator step for where that knowledge comes from.
#
# THE DOMAIN DOES THE CHECKING — this script does not. It used to
# dispatch `Clearance::Start`/`Passed`/`Failed` directly, which was a
# shortcut around two policies the chapter had ALREADY declared for
# exactly this (`ClearOnPass`/`RefuseOnFail`, `quality_control.bluebook`'s
# own foot), leaving them dead code forever. What this script does now:
# `Clearance.Start`, to put the commit on the record — still a direct
# dispatch, because starting a run is this script's own decision, not an
# external fact — and then `Clearance.CI.Run`, the ASK the chapter's own
# `CI` port declares (`quality_control.hecksagon`). `GithubChecks`
# (`qa/adapters/github_checks.rb`) is what actually shells to `gh` from
# there and answers or refuses; `ClearOnPass`/`RefuseOnFail` are what turn
# that into `Clearance::Passed`/`Failed`, for real, the same way
# `Ticket.Raise` already lets `FileWhenSubmitted` drive `IssueTracker`
# rather than filing anything by hand. This script's own remaining job:
# decide WHICH commits are worth asking about at all, and read back what
# the ledger recorded once it has answered.
#
#   bin/qa_pr_check
#
# WHAT THIS REUSES RATHER THAN REINVENTS: the `gh` CLI, already this
# repository's own way of talking to GitHub (`.claude/skills/hecks_qa/
# SKILL.md`'s own `gh pr view`/`gh pr checks` examples) — nothing here
# opens a second HTTP client or re-derives a token. `gh pr view <n>
# --json` is how this script reads a tracked PR's own current state and
# head commit, by number; `gh pr checks <n> --json` is still what decides
# whether a PR is even worth asking the ledger about yet (see "PENDING
# STAYS OUTSIDE THE PORT" below); `GithubChecks` reaches `gh` on its own,
# by commit, for the verdict itself.
#
# PENDING STAYS OUTSIDE THE PORT, ON PURPOSE. `Clearance`'s own two
# endings are `Passed`/`Failed` — final, and minted once per commit
# forever (see `Clearance`'s own header). There is no "ask me again
# later" a `refuses` could mean without lying: refusing "checks are still
# running" would read exactly like refusing "checks failed," and
# `BugCiWatch` cannot tell those apart. So this script still checks
# whether a PR's checks have finished BEFORE it ever starts a `Clearance`
# for that commit — the one decision that has to happen out here, because
# the port's own vocabulary has nowhere to put it.
#
# ONE CLEARANCE PER COMMIT, MINTED ONCE — the same reading `Clearance`'s
# own header comment gives: "a commit's CI result doesn't change once
# settled... a later commit simply has none of its own." So a commit that
# already has a `Clearance` (green OR red) is never re-asked — this is
# NOT a staleness check with a window, on purpose: CI re-running against
# the exact same sha would answer the exact same question a second time,
# and a `Clearance` already answers it. The thing that changes over time
# is which COMMIT a PR's branch points at (a push mints a new sha,
# therefore a new, still-unminted `Clearance` to check next run), never
# what an already-recorded commit's own run said — which is exactly why
# this script reads the LIVE head commit from `gh pr view` every run
# rather than trusting `Patch.commit` (frozen at `Open`, useful as
# provenance, not as this run's own question).
#
# THE LOOP CLOSES FROM THIS END TOO. `Patch`'s own lifecycle is
# `opened` → `merged`/`closed`, however GitHub reports it — the same
# `raised` → `filed`/`closed` shape `Ticket` already keeps for an issue.
# Nothing in this repository is told the moment a PR merges or closes, so
# this script is what asks: a `Patch` GitHub already settled is retired
# here (`Patch.Merge`/`Patch.Close`) before this run ever spends a `gh
# pr checks` call on it, which is also what keeps `Patch.Open` — the
# worklist itself — from growing without bound.
#
# EXIT CODES — the only contract an agent (or a loop) needs to read:
#   0  nothing to act on — no PRs tracked as open at all, or every one
#      already has a recorded Clearance (or its checks are still pending,
#      not yet a verdict) and nothing new was dispatched.
#   1  an operational error — `gh` was not reachable at all, a tracked
#      PR's own head does not look like a sha, or the ledger itself
#      would not boot. Fix the actual problem; there is nothing here for
#      an agent to pick up as QA work.
#   2  FOUND A NEWLY-RED PR — a `Clearance::Failed` was dispatched this
#      run for a commit a `Bug` in this ledger claims to have fixed,
#      which means `BugCiWatch` just fired `Bug.Regress` for real. Full
#      detail (which Bug, which PR, which commit, which checks failed) is
#      on stdout above the exit. An agent picks up the investigation from
#      there — same judgment SKILL.md's own "on a surprising check"
#      section already describes for a sweep's own find: self-contained
#      fix with a regression test and a draft PR, or leave the (now
#      re-opened) Bug for something bigger. Never re-verify or re-close
#      this script's own way; that is exactly the judgment step it does
#      not have.
#
# WHAT THIS SCRIPT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: decide whether a red run
# means the fix was wrong, judge which failing check matters, write a new
# regression test, open a second PR, record a NEW `Patch` for one it does
# not already know about (that is the orchestrator's own job, at the
# moment a PR is opened — see `Patch.Open`'s own comment), or compute a
# pass/fail verdict of its own — that last one used to happen here and
# now happens behind the port instead. It records what the ledger says
# once asked and stops — the process manager already turned a red answer
# into a `Bug.Regress` by the time this script's own exit code says so;
# anything past that is the same investigation `bin/qa_sweep`'s own "on a
# surprising check" judgment already covers, reused rather than restated
# here.

$LOAD_PATH.unshift File.expand_path("../lib", __dir__)

require "hecks"
# ADR 0033 — see bin/run's own identical require: the era/lineage
# subsystem no longer loads with core, and this ledger's `persisted_by
# "Heki"` binding needs it explicitly.
require "hecks/ports/persistence/plugins/era"
require "json"
require "open3"

ROOT       = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
DOMAIN_DIR = File.join(ROOT, "qa/bluebook")

EXIT_OK        = 0
EXIT_ERROR     = 1
EXIT_NEWLY_RED = 2

USAGE = "usage: bin/qa_pr_check".freeze

SHA_PATTERN = /\A[0-9a-fA-F]{7,40}\z/

unless ARGV.empty?
  if %w[-h --help].include?(ARGV.first)
    puts USAGE
    exit EXIT_OK
  end
  abort "#{USAGE}\nthis script takes no arguments — it checks every PR QualityControl::Patch tracks as open, every time"
end

# ── talk to gh, once per call, never guessing at its own exit codes ────
#
# `--json` ALREADY MEANS "I am reading this programmatically" — gh's own
# special exit codes (8 for "checks pending", among others) are reserved
# for its interactive/watch modes and do not fire once `--json` is given;
# confirmed live rather than assumed (`gh pr checks <n> --json ...` exits
# 0 whether every check passed or several failed). So this reads the
# `bucket` field itself for the real verdict and treats gh's own exit
# code as answering only one question: did the CALL succeed at all.
def gh(*)
  Open3.capture3("gh", *)
end

# ── boot the ledger for real ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE REAL `CI` PORT COMES BOUND — `qa/adapters/github_checks.{adapter,
# rb}` is what `Folder#load_project` picks up during this boot (see that
# file's own header for why no line here has to name it), binding
# `GithubChecks` to the port `quality_control.hecksagon` declares. Boot
# this domain any other way (`spec/quality_control_spec.rb`'s own inline
# hecksagon, `bin/model_check`'s static-only load) and the port stays
# exactly as dormant as `IssueTracker`'s still is — real binding is a
# property of THIS boot, not of the chapter.

runtime = begin
  Hecks.boot(DOMAIN_DIR)
rescue StandardError => e
  abort "the QualityControl ledger did not boot — fix the ledger itself before checking PRs against it " \
        "(#{e.class}: #{e.message})"
end

def q(runtime, name, **args) = runtime.query("QualityControl::#{name}", **args)

# `field(row, :commit, :value)`, NOT `row.dig(:commit, :value)` — a query
# row's own field values are `Hecks::Runtime::Value` objects, which answer
# `[]` (the same reader the facade and every spec in this repository
# already use) but not `Hash#dig`. Confirmed live, not assumed: `.dig`
# raises `TypeError: Hecks::Runtime::Value does not have #dig method` the
# first time this script ever ran against a real boot.
def field(row, *path)
  path.reduce(row) { |held, key| held.nil? ? nil : held[key] }
end

def clearance_for_commit(runtime, sha)
  q(runtime, "Clearance.All").find { |row| field(row, :commit, :value) == sha }
end

# ── every PR the ledger itself tracks as open — no search, anywhere ────

patches = q(runtime, "Patch.Open")

if patches.empty?
  puts "no PRs tracked as open by QualityControl::Patch — nothing to check"
  exit EXIT_OK
end

# THE LOOP CLOSES FROM THIS END. `Patch`'s own lifecycle only moves off
# `opened` when something tells it to — nothing in this repository is
# notified the moment GitHub merges or closes a PR, so this is the one
# place that asks and records what it hears, before spending another `gh`
# call on a PR that is already done. Returns whether it retired one.
def retire_if_settled?(view, number, label, runtime)
  case view[:state]
  when "MERGED"
    runtime.dispatch("QualityControl::Patch.Merge", id: number)
    puts "#{label}: merged — retired from the worklist"
    true
  when "CLOSED"
    runtime.dispatch("QualityControl::Patch.Close", id: number)
    puts "#{label}: closed without merging — retired from the worklist"
    true
  else
    false
  end
end

# THE ONE PLACE THIS SCRIPT STILL LOOKS AT `gh` ITSELF — deciding whether
# a commit is even worth asking the ledger about yet. See this file's own
# header, "PENDING STAYS OUTSIDE THE PORT." Returns whether the checks
# have settled (a verdict is worth asking `Clearance.CI.Run` for);
# `false` means this run has nothing left to do for this PR, and why is
# already on stdout/`operational_errors`.
def checks_settled?(number, label, bug_ref, sha, operational_errors)
  checks_out, checks_err, checks_status = gh("pr", "checks", number.to_s,
                                             "--json", "name,state,bucket,workflow")
  unless checks_status.success?
    operational_errors << "#{label}: gh pr checks failed — #{checks_err.strip.empty? ? checks_out.strip : checks_err.strip}"
    return false
  end

  checks = JSON.parse(checks_out, symbolize_names: true)
  if checks.empty?
    puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: gh reports no checks at all yet — skipping this run"
    return false
  end

  if checks.any? { |c| c[:bucket] == "pending" }
    puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: checks still running — skipping this run"
    return false
  end

  true
end

# ONE PATCH, START TO FINISH — pulled out of the loop below so the loop
# itself reads as "for every tracked-open PR, check it" rather than
# burying that shape under every early-exit this one PR's own checking
# needs. Appends to `newly_red`/`operational_errors` in place rather than
# returning a verdict to fold in, the same reporting shape `bin/qa_sweep`'s
# own per-seed loop uses.
def check_one_patch(patch_row, runtime, newly_red, operational_errors)
  number  = field(patch_row, :number, :value)
  bug_ref = field(patch_row, :bug)
  label   = "PR ##{number} (#{field(patch_row, :branch, :value)})"

  view_out, view_err, view_status = gh("pr", "view", number.to_s,
                                       "--json", "state,headRefOid,url")
  unless view_status.success?
    operational_errors << "#{label}: gh pr view failed — #{view_err.strip.empty? ? view_out.strip : view_err.strip}"
    return
  end

  view = JSON.parse(view_out, symbolize_names: true)
  return if retire_if_settled?(view, number, label, runtime)

  sha = view[:headRefOid].to_s
  unless sha.match?(SHA_PATTERN)
    operational_errors << "#{label}: head commit #{sha.inspect} does not look like a sha — skipping"
    return
  end

  if (existing = clearance_for_commit(runtime, sha))
    puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: already cleared (#{existing[:status]}) — settled once, not re-checked"
    return
  end

  return unless checks_settled?(number, label, bug_ref, sha, operational_errors)

  ask_the_ledger(sha, label, bug_ref, number, runtime, newly_red)
end

# THE ASK, AND THE VERDICT READ BACK — this is the whole replacement for
# the old `record_clearance`, which used to compute pass/fail itself and
# dispatch `Clearance::Passed`/`Failed` by hand. `Clearance.Start` still
# happens here (starting a run is this script's own decision); everything
# past it is `Clearance.CI.Run` — the `CI` port's own `asks "Run"` — and
# the two policies the chapter already declared. `GithubChecks` answers
# or refuses from what `gh` says about THIS commit; `ClearOnPass`/
# `RefuseOnFail` are what turn that into `Clearance::Passed`/`Failed`,
# synchronously, before `runtime.dispatch` returns — so `Clearance.find`
# right after already sees the settled record, same as `record_clearance`
# used to leave one.
def ask_the_ledger(sha, label, bug_ref, number, runtime, newly_red)
  QualityControl::Clearance.start!(commit: { value: sha })
  runtime.dispatch("QualityControl::Clearance.CI.Run", commit: sha)

  cleared = QualityControl::Clearance.find(sha)
  summary = cleared.summary.to_h[:value]

  if cleared.status == "green"
    puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: CLEARED — #{summary}"
    return
  end

  puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: RED — #{summary}"
  newly_red << { pr: number, bug: bug_ref, commit: sha, refusal: summary }
end

newly_red = []
operational_errors = []

patches.each { |patch_row| check_one_patch(patch_row, runtime, newly_red, operational_errors) }

# ── report and exit ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

unless operational_errors.empty?
  puts
  puts "operational error(s):"
  operational_errors.each { |message| puts "  #{message}" }
end

if newly_red.any?
  puts
  puts "=" * 72
  puts "FOUND #{newly_red.length} NEWLY-RED PR(S) — BugCiWatch already dispatched Bug.Regress for each."
  puts "An agent picks up the investigation from here: the same judgment"
  puts "SKILL.md's own \"on a surprising check\" section describes for a sweep's"
  puts "own find — self-contained fix with a regression test and a draft PR,"
  puts "or leave the bug for something bigger. Never re-verify from this script."
  puts "=" * 72
  newly_red.each do |finding|
    puts
    puts "bug:     #{finding[:bug]}"
    puts "pr:      ##{finding[:pr]}"
    puts "commit:  #{finding[:commit]}"
    puts "refusal: #{finding[:refusal]}"
  end
  exit EXIT_NEWLY_RED
end

exit EXIT_ERROR if operational_errors.any?

puts
puts "clean — every PR QualityControl::Patch tracks as open is either already cleared, still running, or was retired this run."
exit EXIT_OK
